<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://uhf.microsoft.com/statics/20260910.10.32/css/style-By05NU7M.css" /><script type="application/ld+json" class="nlweb-schema">
{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "name": "Today, we’re proud to introduce Maia 200, a breakthrough inference accelerator engineered to dramatically improve the economics of AI token generation.",
    "description": "Microsoft unveils Maia 200, a next-generation AI inference accelerator featuring TSMC’s 3nm process, advanced memory systems, and industry-leading performance and efficiency. Maia 200 is designed to power large-scale AI workloads, including OpenAI’s GPT-5.2, with seamless Azure integration and a developer-focused SDK preview.",
    "abstract": "Microsoft launches Maia 200, a high-performance AI inference accelerator delivering breakthrough efficiency and performance for large-scale AI workloads.",
    "author": {
        "@type": "Person",
        "name": "Scott Guthrie"
    },
    "keywords": [
        "Featured",
        "The Official Microsoft Blog"
    ],
    "datePublished": "2026-01-26T08:00:30",
    "thumbnailUrl": "https://blogs.microsoft.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/Maia200_header.jpg",
    "articleBody": "Microsoft has introduced Maia 200, a breakthrough AI inference accelerator built on TSMC’s 3nm process, featuring native FP8/FP4 tensor cores, a redesigned memory system with 216GB HBM3e at 7 TB/s, and 272MB of on-chip SRAM. Delivering three times the FP4 performance of Amazon Trainium 3 and outperforming Google’s TPU v7 in FP8, Maia 200 offers 30% better performance per dollar than Microsoft’s previous generation hardware. Deployed initially in the US Central datacenter near Des Moines, with US West 3 in Arizona to follow, Maia 200 will support major workloads including OpenAI’s GPT-5.2, Microsoft Foundry, and Microsoft 365 Copilot. Its architecture includes a two-tier Ethernet-based scale-up network, custom transport layer, and up to 2.8 TB/s bidirectional bandwidth per accelerator, supporting clusters of up to 6,144 units. Each Maia 200 chip delivers over 10 petaFLOPS (FP4) and 5 petaFLOPS (FP8) within a 750W SoC envelope, and is optimized for large-scale, low-precision compute. The Maia SDK, now in preview, provides tools for model building and optimization, including PyTorch integration, a Triton compiler, and a low-level programming language. Microsoft’s cloud-native development approach enabled rapid deployment from first silicon to datacenter, halving the usual timeline. As part of a multi-generational roadmap, Maia 200 sets new benchmarks for AI infrastructure, with developers, startups, and academics invited to join the SDK preview. Additional resources, images, and details are available on Microsoft’s official sites.",
    "customData": {
        "topics": [
            "AI",
            "Inference Acceleration",
            "Cloud Infrastructure",
            "Synthetic Data",
            "Model Optimization"
        ],
        "products": [
            "Azure",
            "Copilot"
        ],
        "persons": []
    }
}
</script>
